                                    <h2><span class="shaft"><img id="1108-0" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_c.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Cardioid" title="Cardioid" ></span>Cascade Microphones V55</h2>
                    
                    <h2 class="sub">                    Cardioid Tube Condenser Microphone</h2>
                    <p>The Cascade Valve 55, aka V55, was an early large-diaphragm tube mic from Cascade Microphones, produced from 2004 through about 2007. It had a transformer-coupled output and a fixed Cardioid pickup pattern.</p><p>The capsule was a 35mm center-terminated design.</p><p>The tube was a Mullard 12AT7WA/CV4024, a high-quality, low-noise tube manufactured in Great Britain. Presumably these were installed by Cascade Microphones staff in the US, as the rest of the microphone was manufactured in China.</p><p>The mic had a -10dB pad and a high-pass filter. Its amplifier circuit was relatively quiet, at 16dBA, and used a transformer-coupled output.</p><p><a rel="shadowbox" href="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images//mic_extras/cascade/V55-PSU.jpg"><img src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/mic_extras/cascade/V55-PSU-sm.jpg" class="thumb shaft" alt="V55 PSU" width="133" /></a>The V55 shipped with a &#8220;vintage style&#8221; switchable (115V/220V) power supply, 8-pin screw-on cable, a microfiber cleaning cloth, an elastic shockmount with spare elastics, a wooden storage case for the mic, and an aluminum flight case.</p><p><span style="font: 9px Verdana,Sans-Serif;">PSU photo credit: Dennis Stone</span><p>The <a href="//recordinghacks.com/microphones/Cascade/V55">Cascade Microphones V55</a> is also known as: Valve 55.</p><p>The mic was released in 2004.</p>
